// ------------------------------------------------------------------ overview
= Overview

AlphaChess learns to play chess from nothing but the rules. There is no opening
book, no endgame tablebase, no handcrafted evaluation function, and no database
of human games. A single neural network is trained against data generated by
searching with an earlier version of itself, and the only external signal in the
entire pipeline is the result of a finished game: win, loss, or draw.

The method is a loop with two halves.

#v(0.3em)
#align(center, block(
  stroke: 0.6pt + luma(160), radius: 3pt, inset: 12pt, width: 92%,
)[
  #set text(size: 9.5pt)
  #grid(
    columns: (1fr, auto, 1fr),
    align: horizon + center,
    gutter: 8pt,
    block(fill: rgb("#eef4f8"), inset: 9pt, radius: 3pt, width: 100%)[
      *Self-play* \
      #text(size: 8.7pt)[
        Play thousands of games against yourself. Every move is chosen by a
        PUCT tree search guided by the current network. Record, for each
        searched position, the search's move distribution and (once the game
        ends) its outcome.
      ]
    ],
    text(size: 16pt)[→],
    block(fill: rgb("#eef4f8"), inset: 9pt, radius: 3pt, width: 100%)[
      *Learning* \
      #text(size: 8.7pt)[
        Fit the network to that data: its policy head towards the search's
        move distribution, its value head towards the game's outcome. Write
        the new weights; the next iteration of self-play uses them.
      ]
    ],
  )
  #v(6pt)
  #text(size: 8.7pt, style: "italic")[
    Each pass is one #emph[iteration]. The network that generates the data is
    always the newest one, so the data improves as the network does.
  ]
])
#v(0.3em)

The engine of improvement is that *search is stronger than the network alone*.
A network evaluated once on a position gives a decent guess at the best move; a
few hundred simulations of tree search using that same network gives a better
one. Training the raw network to imitate its own searched output therefore
moves it towards something it could not yet produce directly --- and the next
search, now guided by a better network, is better still. This is policy
iteration, with a neural network as the function approximator and MCTS as the
policy improvement operator.

