// ------------------------------------------------------------------ input
= Board representation

The network sees a position as a stack of $21$ binary-or-scalar planes of shape
$8 times 8$, i.e. a tensor of shape $(21, 8, 8)$. This is built by
`encode_board()` in `encoding.py`.

== Side-to-move relative orientation

The single most important property of the encoding is that it is *relative to
the side to move*, not absolute. When Black is to move, the board is flipped
vertically and the piece planes are swapped so that "our pieces" always occupy
planes 0--5 and always advance towards increasing rank.

The reason is sample efficiency. Chess is very nearly symmetric under colour
reversal: a position and its mirror have the same evaluation with the sign
flipped, and the same best move reflected. An absolute encoding forces the
network to learn every motif twice --- once for White, once for Black --- from
disjoint halves of the data. A relative encoding lets a single set of filters
serve both, so every training example teaches both colours at once.

#note[
  The flip is an implementation detail *contained inside* `encoding.py`.
  `encode_board()`, `move_to_index()` and `index_to_move()` all take the board
  and apply or undo the orientation themselves, so every caller works in
  ordinary absolute `chess` coordinates. Nothing outside this module has to
  know the network sees a flipped world.
]

The flip itself is a single `arr[:, ::-1, :]` on the assembled stack. Planes
that are spatially constant (the castling flags, the halfmove clock, the
repetition indicators, the ones plane) are unaffected by a vertical flip, so
flipping everything at once is safe and costs one array copy.

== The 21 planes

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
  stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200),
  inset: (x: 6pt, y: 4.5pt),
  align: (center, center, left),
  table.header(
    [*Planes*], [*Values*], [*Contents*],
  ),
  [0--5], [$0/1$], [Our pawns, knights, bishops, rooks, queens, king],
  [6--11], [$0/1$], [Their pawns, knights, bishops, rooks, queens, king],
  [12], [$0/1$], [Our kingside castling right (broadcast over the plane)],
  [13], [$0/1$], [Our queenside castling right],
  [14], [$0/1$], [Their kingside castling right],
  [15], [$0/1$], [Their queenside castling right],
  [16], [$0/1$], [En-passant target square (a single set square, or empty)],
  [17], [$[0,1]$], [Halfmove clock $div 100$, clipped --- progress toward the fifty-move draw],
  [18], [$0/1$], [This position has occurred at least once before],
  [19], [$0/1$], [This position has occurred at least twice before],
  [20], [$1$], [Constant ones],
)

Three of these deserve comment.

*Planes 18--19 make the state Markovian.* Chess as usually stated is not a
Markov decision process on the board alone: the legality of a draw claim, and
therefore the true value of a position, depends on history. Without repetition
planes the network is asked to evaluate positions whose value it cannot
determine from its input, and a search that finds a repetition has no way to
tell the network that a draw is now available. Two planes are enough to
distinguish "new", "seen once" and "seen twice" (the third occurrence being a
claimable draw).

*Plane 20 lets padded convolutions find the edge.* A $3 times 3$ convolution
with zero padding cannot, by itself, distinguish "the board ends here" from
"there are no pieces here". A constant plane of ones gives the filters a signal
that is $1$ inside the board and $0$ in the padding, which makes edge and corner
effects --- rook mobility, king safety on the back rank --- learnable.

*The piece planes are built from bitboards, not a loop.* `_piece_planes()` takes
python-chess's twelve 64-bit masks, concatenates them into 96 little-endian
bytes, and expands them with a single `numpy.unpackbits` call. Bit $i$ of a mask
is square $i$, so the unpacked bits reshape directly to
`[plane, rank, file]`. This replaces a Python loop over `board.piece_map()` and
is the difference between encoding being a rounding error and being a
bottleneck.

== Storage packing

Stored in float32, one position is $21 times 64 times 4 = 5.4$ KB, and a replay
buffer of two million positions would be 10.8 GB of states alone. Every value in
the encoding is either binary or a scaled small rational, so `pack_state()`
quantises to `uint8` and `unpack_state()` restores:

$ "packed" = "round"(x dot 255), quad x = "packed" / 255 $

This is lossless for the binary planes and costs at most $1/255$ of precision on
plane 17, which is itself only a coarse progress indicator. The cost is 1.3 KB
per position instead of 5.4 KB.

