// ------------------------------------------------------------------ moves
= Move representation

The policy head emits a fixed vector of $4672$ logits. That number is
$64 times 73$: for each of the 64 *origin* squares, 73 possible *move types*.
This is the AlphaZero move encoding, and it is chosen so that a move's index
depends on geometry rather than on the particular position --- the same index
means the same thing everywhere on the board.

$ "index"(m) = "from" times 73 + "plane"(m) $

== The 73 planes

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
  stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200),
  inset: (x: 6pt, y: 4.5pt),
  align: (center, center, left),
  table.header([*Planes*], [*Count*], [*Move type*]),
  [0--55], [56], [
    Queen-like slides: 8 directions (N, NE, E, SE, S, SW, W, NW) × 7 distances
    (1--7). Plane $= d times 7 + (n - 1)$.
  ],
  [56--63], [8], [Knight jumps, one plane per offset.],
  [64--72], [9], [
    Underpromotions: 3 file deltas (left, straight, right) × 3 pieces
    (knight, bishop, rook).
  ],
)

Everything that is not a knight jump or an underpromotion is a queen-like slide,
including king moves (distance 1), *castling* (encoded as the king's two-square
move), *en passant* (a diagonal pawn slide), and *queen promotion* (a pawn's
one-square advance or capture onto the back rank). Queen promotion needs no
plane of its own because a pawn reaching the back rank can only promote, and
promoting to a queen is the overwhelming default; the three underpromotion
planes exist precisely because that default is occasionally wrong.

== Orientation, again

`move_to_index()` applies the same vertical flip as the encoder before computing
the index:

```python
if board.turn != chess.WHITE:
    from_sq ^= 56          # rank r -> rank 7-r, file unchanged
    to_sq   ^= 56
```

The `^ 56` trick works because a square index is `rank * 8 + file`, so XOR-ing
the top three bits with `111` maps rank $r$ to rank $7-r$ and leaves the file
alone. The consequence is the one that matters: *a position and its colour
mirror map corresponding moves to identical policy indices*, so the policy head
shares all its learning across colours exactly as the board encoding does.

== Coverage

The encoding is not obviously complete, and `test_encoding.py` checks that it is:
every legal move, across openings, random playouts, promotions, underpromotions,
castling and en passant, must survive a round trip through `move_to_index()` and
`index_to_move()`. Of the 4672 slots, a large fraction are never legal from any
position (a knight jumping off the board, a promotion from the middle of the
board); these simply never receive prior mass, because the search masks the
policy to the legal moves before using it.

