// ------------------------------------------------------------------ selfplay
= Self-play

Self-play is the wall-clock bottleneck of the whole method, and most of this
repository's engineering is here. The naive implementation --- one game, one
simulation at a time, one position per forward pass --- leaves a modern GPU
about 99% idle.

== Batching: one leaf per game per step

The structural trick is to run many games *concurrently* and interleave their
searches. Each game advances its own tree by exactly one simulation per step,
and every game's leaf position for that step is collected into a single batch:

#align(center, block(stroke: 0.6pt + luma(160), radius: 3pt, inset: 11pt, width: 92%)[
  #set text(size: 9.3pt)
  #set par(justify: false)
  *for each step:* \
  #h(1em) 1. for every active game: descend its tree to a leaf (CPU) \
  #h(1em) 2. stack all those leaves into one $(G, 21, 8, 8)$ batch \
  #h(1em) 3. one forward pass (GPU) \
  #h(1em) 4. for every game: expand its leaf and back up (CPU) \
  #h(1em) 5. any game whose budget is spent plays its move and may finish
])

With `--games-in-flight 1024`, the network sees batches of about a thousand
positions instead of one. Since a GPU's throughput at batch 1 and at batch 1024
differ by orders of magnitude, this single change is most of the speedup: a
$128 times 10$ network runs at $tilde.op 94$k positions/s at batch 512,
$tilde.op 121$k at 1024, and $tilde.op 127$k at 2048.

The cost is memory: each in-flight game holds its own search tree and its own
accumulated training examples, so `--games-in-flight` is bounded by host RAM.

== Pools: overlapping CPU and GPU

Batching alone still alternates: the CPU descends while the GPU idles, then the
GPU computes while the CPU idles. `--pools` runs several independent groups of
games so that one pool's tree search overlaps the next pool's forward pass.

Two pools suffice in principle; three is the default because it also absorbs the
jitter from a pool that momentarily has few active games. More than three just
fragments the batches. The knob interacts with `--games-per-iter`: a pool refills
a finished game with a fresh one until its quota is exhausted, after which the
batch decays as the last games drain, so keeping games-per-iter well above
$"pools" times "games-in-flight"$ (16000 vs 3072 in the default recipe) keeps
that tail a small share of the iteration.

== Playout-cap randomisation

Most of a game's search budget buys very little. The position is not close, and
a 200-simulation search picks the move a 50-simulation search would. Playout-cap
randomisation --- from KataGo #footnote[Wu, D. J. (2019). #emph[Accelerating
Self-Play Learning in Go].] --- exploits this asymmetry between the two things a
self-play game produces.

A game produces *policy targets* (one per searched ply, and only worth recording
if the search was strong) and *one value target* (the game's result, which is
worth exactly as much regardless of how carefully the moves were chosen). So:

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200),
  inset: (x: 7pt, y: 5pt),
  table.header([*Ply type*], [*Treatment*]),
  [Full (probability `--full-search-prob`, default 0.25)],
  [
    Full `--simulations` budget, root Dirichlet noise, *and* the resulting
    visit distribution is recorded as a training example.
  ],
  [Fast (the other 75%)],
  [
    Only `--fast-simulations` (default 50), no noise, *no training example
    recorded*. These plies exist purely to move the game along to a result.
  ],
)

Measured over 400 complete games, this takes a ply from 179 evaluated positions
to 77 --- so the same GPU-hour finishes *2.2× as many games*, and therefore 2.2×
as many game outcomes to learn a value function from, while every policy target
it records still comes from a full-strength search. Subtree reuse compounds it:
a fast ply often already has more carried-over visits than its budget calls for,
and costs a single simulation.

The accounting consequence is that an iteration records roughly 4× fewer
positions per game, which the `--sample-reuse` rule (§7.4) accounts for
automatically.

== Resignation

Playing out a hopeless position to checkmate is wasted GPU time. When the
mover's best root value stays at or below `--resign-threshold` (default $-0.90$)
for two consecutive plies, the game is resigned and scored as a loss.

The danger is obvious: a resignation threshold that is too aggressive
mislabels positions that were actually saveable, and poisons the value target.
So a fraction of games --- `--resign-disable-fraction`, default 10% --- is
played out with resignation *suppressed*, purely so the engine can measure how
often it *would* have resigned a game it went on to draw or win. That
false-positive rate is printed every iteration. If it climbs above about 5%, the
threshold is too loose and should be lowered.

== The native search core

Profiling the pure-Python engine put the time as follows:

#table(
  columns: (1fr, auto),
  stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200),
  inset: (x: 7pt, y: 4.5pt),
  align: (left, right),
  table.header([*Where self-play time went (Python engine)*], [*Share*]),
  [python-chess legal move generation], [$tilde.op$40%],
  [Move → policy index], [$tilde.op$14%],
  [Board → tensor encoding], [$tilde.op$10%],
  [Tree descent (push, PUCT select, backup)], [$tilde.op$17%],
)

None of that is GPU work. All of it is Python doing bookkeeping, and the card
sat idle waiting for it. So the board, move generation, the encoding and the
PUCT tree were all moved into a small C extension (`alpha_chess/native/`):

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  stroke: none,
  inset: (x: 6pt, y: 4pt),
  fill: (_, y) => if calc.odd(y) { rgb("#f6f8fa") },
  [`bitboard.h`], [attack tables and runtime-generated magic bitboards],
  [`position.h`], [position, Zobrist hashing, make-move, legal move generation],
  [`encode.h`], [the 21-plane encoding and 4672-way index, matching `encoding.py`],
  [`mcts.h`], [arena-allocated PUCT trees and the self-play game driver],
  [`fastchess.c`], [CPython bindings; releases the GIL for the whole search step],
)

The extension is compiled on first use --- `native.py` tries `cc`, `gcc`,
`clang`, then the pip-installed `ziglang` --- so no system compiler and no build
step is required of the user, and a host without one silently falls back to the
Python engine.

#note[
  *Result.* $tilde.op 102$k evaluated positions/s against $tilde.op 27$k for the
  pure-Python engine on the same hardware: *3.8× the raw search throughput*, and
  with playout caps, *8.5× the games per hour*. The bottleneck moves off the CPU
  and onto the GPU, which is where it should be --- the card now runs at its
  power limit. A week-long run becomes a long weekend.
]

The obligation this creates is correctness: a second chess implementation is a
second chance to be subtly wrong. `test_native.py` runs *perft* on the six
standard positions (\~194M nodes between them, covering castling, en passant,
promotion, discovered check, pins and stalemate) and diffs the native legal-move
set, move-to-index mapping and encoded planes against python-chess and
`encoding.py` over thousands of positions from random games --- including
en-passant positions specifically, the one feature where the two encoders could
plausibly disagree.

