// ------------------------------------------------------------------ eval
= Measuring strength

`evaluate.py` plays a checkpoint against an opponent over a series of games with
alternating colours and reports W/D/L, score, and an Elo difference:

$ Delta"Elo" = -400 log_10 (1/S - 1) $

where $S$ is the score in $[0, 1]$, clamped at $1 \/ (2 n)$ so a whitewash gives
a finite estimate that tightens as the match lengthens.

#table(
  columns: (auto, 1fr),
  stroke: 0.4pt + luma(200),
  inset: (x: 7pt, y: 4.5pt),
  table.header([*Opponent*], [*What it tells you*]),
  [`random`], [Sanity only. Any trained network should crush it.],
  [`material`], [A greedy material-counting baseline. The first real rung.],
  [`model:PATH`], [Another checkpoint of your own --- measures *improvement*.],
  [`uci:PATH`], [An external engine (e.g. Stockfish) --- the only path to a comparable number.],
)

#note[
  *The Elo number is relative to the opponent, not absolute.* Beating `material`
  by 300 points says nothing about FIDE rating. Only a calibrated external
  engine anchors a comparable figure --- and even then, note that engines
  enforce a floor on strength limiting (Stockfish 16's `UCI_Elo` will not go
  below 1320; below that, use `--uci-skill`, which reaches Skill Level 0), and
  that a strength-limited engine is not a human of the same nominal rating. It
  plays weaker moves but still never hangs a piece.
]

Games are decorrelated by `--opening-plies` random opening moves per
colour-swapped pair, so two deterministic players still produce varied games
rather than repeating one line.

