= What this is and is not

This is a faithful, runnable implementation of the AlphaZero method, engineered
so that the loop closes on a single consumer GPU in days rather than on a TPU
pod in hours. The reference work used 5,000 first-generation TPUs for self-play
and 64 second-generation TPUs for training; the target here is one RTX 3090 and
a practical ceiling around 1500--2000 Elo.

Everything that makes that feasible is a throughput decision, not an algorithmic
shortcut: batching thousands of concurrent games into one forward pass, moving
the search into C so the GPU is the bottleneck, spending the search budget where
it buys game outcomes rather than uniformly, and storing the replay buffer
compactly enough that a useful window fits in RAM. The learning algorithm
itself --- PUCT with network priors, visit-count policy targets, game-outcome
value targets, and a combined cross-entropy plus MSE loss --- is unchanged.

What it is not is a promise of grandmaster strength. The scaling is real but so
is the gap: AlphaZero's published result is several orders of magnitude more
compute away.
